WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.830
Loki might just be Marvel's most beloved villain…
or at least Marvel's most beloved villain

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.129
that's still alive.

00:00:06.129 --> 00:00:10.350
On the eve of Tom Hiddleston's spinoff, we
review his back-stabbing, Hulk-wielding, treacherous

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:11.350
MCU behavior.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.510
Anybody with a sibling would probably admit
there have been times when they've at least

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.420
idly considered murder — but they're not
serious.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.460
Loki, on the other hand, has indulged in the
desire to end his brother's life, not even

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.340
once, but on multiple occasions.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.800
He seems to have honed this murderous impulse
from a young age, beginning with the snake

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.210
stabbing incident.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.820
Towards the end of "Ragnarok," when the newly
formed Revengers are busy making plans to

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.000
free Asgard from the diabolical grip of Hela,
they discuss their plans, at which point the

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:38.820
group is interrupted by Loki.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.010
Thor casually throws out the fact that his
little brother has tried to kill him many

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.079
times before.

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:47.142
“He transformed into a snake and he knows
I love snakes so I went to pick up the snake

00:00:47.142 --> 00:00:51.019
to admire it and he transformed back into
himself and he was like ‘byah!’ and then

00:00:51.019 --> 00:00:52.140
he stabbed me.”

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.579
Kid Loki literally lured his brother into
a trap and tried to kill him.

00:00:55.579 --> 00:00:59.670
We've all heard the phrase "boys will be boys,"
but this story sounds a bit extreme, even

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:02.100
by the outsized standards of Asgardian myth.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.890
Let's not split hairs: Thor's problems in
his first standalone film were largely due

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:08.979
to the God of Thunder's own personal issues.

00:01:08.979 --> 00:01:12.850
His headstrong, stubborn behavior got the
Son of Odin banished to Earth in a hot second

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:17.100
— after literally starting an interplanetary
conflict between Jotunheim and Asgard.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.950
That said, you don't have to peel back the
curtain too far to see that it was actually

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:24.760
Loki who was pulling the strings behind the
whole situation the entire time.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:28.640
The mischievous brother kicked things off
by letting the Frost Giants into Asgard before

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.150
the kingdom's big coronation ceremony.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:35.580
After that, he was right there by Thor's side,
convincing his unruly brother to enact vigilante

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.869
justice on Frost Giant leader Laufey and his
oversized minions.

00:01:38.869 --> 00:01:42.900
He also ensured that their father Odin found
them, saved them ... and then kicked Thor

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:46.240
off Asgard as punishment for his impetuous
crimes.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.840
From there, Loki took his brother's place,
informed Thor his parents had rejected him,

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.850
and sent the massive, all-but-indestructible
being known as the Destroyer to kill Thor.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:58.369
In fact, the plot of the entire first film
has Loki enacting one diabolical scheme after

00:01:58.369 --> 00:02:00.869
another, pulling strings from start to finish.

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:04.439
Loki's treatment of his brother in "Thor"
was pretty appalling.

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:08.159
But it pales in comparison to the collateral
damage the quarreling brother created in the

00:02:08.159 --> 00:02:10.779
wake of his bid for the Asgardian throne in
Thor's absence.

00:02:10.779 --> 00:02:15.110
At the end of the first "Thor" film, Loki
races out to the Bifrost, where he initiates

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:19.049
the rainbow bridge and sends it hurtling directly
into Jotunheim itself.

00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:22.650
When Thor arrives on the scene, he pleads
with his brother, saying that channeling dimensional

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.799
energy would rip the planet apart.

00:02:24.799 --> 00:02:28.629
This, in many ways, might be Loki's most despicable
act of all.

00:02:28.629 --> 00:02:32.230
Whatever the outcome, it marks the point where
his recklessly destructive mischievousness

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:36.919
boiled over onto innocent bystanders — like,
lots of innocent bystanders.

00:02:36.919 --> 00:02:40.469
As the Bifrost rips through the home planet
of the Frost Giants, buildings are leveled

00:02:40.469 --> 00:02:42.959
and crowds are shown running for their lives.

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:48.340
Many are crushed in plain view; numerous others
most likely met a similarly dismal fate offscreen.

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:52.590
Such rampant destruction in the name of power
and family quarreling isn't just wrong — it's

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:53.590
sickening.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:56.780
Loki's blatant disregard for an entire planet
is scary.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:00.319
No matter how charming the character might
be at times, and how easy it is to root for

00:03:00.319 --> 00:03:04.650
him when he decides to play the hero at certain
MCU moments, it's important to remember that

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:09.189
he is also very capable of careless wanton
destruction — and massive collateral damage.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.790
Pinning the blame for the Chitauri invasion
of Earth entirely on the God of Mischief can

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:17.029
be a bit of a point of division for pro-Loki
Marvel fans.

00:03:17.029 --> 00:03:20.760
And admittedly, in Loki's defense, the God
of Mischief was actually under the influence

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.739
of the Mind Stone throughout the invasion.

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:26.559
This little factoid started as a fan theory,
building up steam until it was confirmed by

00:03:26.559 --> 00:03:28.519
the powers that be at Marvel Studios.

00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:32.469
They even updated their official page for
Loki with the explanatory line that, while

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:36.639
he has been known to influence others with
the Scepter holding the Infinity Stone, "unbeknownst

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:41.339
to him, the Scepter was also influencing him,
fueling his hatred over his brother Thor and

00:03:41.339 --> 00:03:43.029
the inhabitants of Earth."

00:03:43.029 --> 00:03:46.909
That's a pretty good case for Loki's at least
partial innocence during the Earth invasion

00:03:46.909 --> 00:03:49.510
catastrophe that leads to the big set piece
in "Avengers."

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:54.279
However, it does not excuse one critical piece
of the story: Loki was the guy who went to

00:03:54.279 --> 00:03:55.779
Thanos in the first place.

00:03:55.779 --> 00:03:59.010
The Son of Odin was certainly in his right
mind when he showed up knocking at the Mad

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.589
Titan's door, asking for a shortcut to power.

00:04:01.589 --> 00:04:05.849
If any of us helped a murderer, there's a
good chance we'd be hearing from the police;

00:04:05.849 --> 00:04:07.849
Loki helped the guy who murdered half the
galaxy.

00:04:08.849 --> 00:04:12.939
Early in "Avengers," Loki allows himself to
be captured and placed in confinement on the

00:04:12.939 --> 00:04:13.939
Helicarrier.

00:04:13.939 --> 00:04:17.729
As the Avengers make their first, failed attempt
to coalesce into a team, Loki remains happily

00:04:17.729 --> 00:04:20.120
contained, waiting for his chance to strike.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:24.030
When the time comes, Loki's lackeys arrive
on the scene, at which point all Asgardian

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.120
hell breaks loose.

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.419
Part of that process is the unleashing of
the Hulk, as Banner loses control and begins

00:04:29.419 --> 00:04:30.979
to tear the ship apart.

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:35.020
The Avengers manage to contain the damage
and keep the ship afloat, but in the chaos,

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.780
lives are lost — including the death of
beloved S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.020
agent Phil Coulson at the hand of Loki himself.

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:44.419
While the "influenced by another being" argument
does still hold up through all of this, it

00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:47.520
doesn't change the fact that Loki is clearly
enjoying himself.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.400
It's hard to argue that he wouldn't have experienced
similar joy without the Mind Stone at play.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:56.810
To be fair, this item on the list of Loki's
all-time worst MCU misdeeds was an accident.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:00.889
But this is an examination of Loki's worst
moves, and sometimes those can be accidental

00:05:00.889 --> 00:05:04.210
— like in "The Dark World," when he helped
cause his adoptive mother's death.

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:08.370
When the dark elf Kurse neglects to free Loki
from prison, the God of Mischief gives him

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.520
some pointed advice:

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:17.169
"you might want to take the stairs to the
left."

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:21.150
But spitefully giving Kurse directions ultimately
blew up in Loki's face painfully when the

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:24.080
monster kills Frigga while she is attempting
to defend Jane Foster.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.370
While his devious comment ends up causing
trouble for others, too, in this particular

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:32.400
instance Loki had no one to blame but himself
for the emotional price of his backhanded

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.400
deed.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.370
After the events of "The Dark World," Loki
spends a number of years masquerading as his

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:38.370
father.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:41.629
The fact that the son is stealing his own
father's identity is bad enough to land on

00:05:41.629 --> 00:05:45.810
the extra naughty list — but don't forget,
Loki also keeps Thor in the dark by encouraging

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:50.530
his brother to stay off-world pursuing his
own quests rather than worrying about Asgard.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:55.100
Once Loki's on the Asgardian throne, he uses
the opportunity to build statues to himself,

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:59.120
indulge his deepest narcissistic tendencies,
and casts a spell over his father that leaves

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:01.389
him in a daze at a retirement home.

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:05.910
Making matters worse, Loki's time as king
of Asgard was most likely a thoroughly pre-planned

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:06.930
ordeal.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:10.530
Fans have long theorized that Loki never had
a kingdom on Earth in mind when he asked Thanos

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.310
to borrow an invasion army.

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:16.129
Some have postulated that the Asgardian prince
may have actually been playing the long game

00:06:16.129 --> 00:06:19.969
by scheming to get captured and brought back
to Asgard, so he could worm his way onto the

00:06:19.969 --> 00:06:21.509
throne one way or another.

00:06:21.509 --> 00:06:25.650
In either case, impersonating a monarch and
ruling in his name definitely earns a place

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:27.930
of distinction on the list of Loki's worst
moments.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:33.330
Accident or not, one of Loki's worst moments
came when he inadvertently opened the door

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.810
for his horrifying sister Hela to get back
to Asgard.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.990
As Hela is unleashed from her prison, she
arrives on the scene, destroys Mjolnir, and

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.449
begins toying with her brothers.

00:06:41.449 --> 00:06:46.150
In response, Loki panics, asking Skurge to
return him and his brother back to Asgard.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:50.419
As the pair are whisked back to Asgard, Hela
hitches a ride and kicks them both out.

00:06:50.419 --> 00:06:53.659
Considering the genocidal, destructive events
that followed in the film, that moment of

00:06:53.659 --> 00:06:56.330
retreat was a very, very bad move.

00:06:56.330 --> 00:07:00.539
It's hard not to pin the blame on Loki for
Hela getting such an easy ride to Asgard.

00:07:01.539 --> 00:07:04.760
After Loki and Thor are thrown out of the
Bifrost in "Ragnarok," they find themselves

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:06.360
on the planet Sakaar.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:10.440
Loki gets there first and by the time Thor
shows up, Loki is in full-fledged self-survival

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:13.860
mode at this point, and refuses to lift a
finger to help his brother.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:17.900
As Thor is prepped for his impending showdown
with the Grandmaster's champion, Loki sits

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.330
in a cushy box seat.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:23.439
Perhaps even worse, he cheers as his brother
gets the same treatment at Hulk's hands as

00:07:23.439 --> 00:07:25.289
Loki suffered in "Avengers."

00:07:25.289 --> 00:07:29.090
Loki betrays his brother again when Thor makes
his jailbreak off the planet, attempting to

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:31.319
turn his brother over to the authorities.

00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:34.970
Granted, Thor is one step ahead, and Loki
may have even made amends with his brother

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:39.259
not long afterward, but all this behavior
was quite reprehensible, even by Loki's standards.

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:43.440
When Loki sees something shiny, he can't keep
his hands off it.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.939
This holds true when he walks past the Tesseract
at the end of "Ragnarok."

00:07:46.939 --> 00:07:50.889
When Loki and Thor save the day by unleashing
Ragnarok by placing the Crown of Surtur on

00:07:50.889 --> 00:07:54.220
the Eternal Flame and defeating Hela, a happy
ending seems in order.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:58.139
"The damage is not too bad, as long as the
foundations are strong...

00:07:58.139 --> 00:08:03.009
Now those foundations are gone.

00:08:03.009 --> 00:08:04.009
Sorry."

00:08:04.009 --> 00:08:07.749
But, as the Asgardian refugees travel through
space, they are confronted by none other than

00:08:07.749 --> 00:08:10.979
Thanos himself in his search for the Infinity
Stones.

00:08:10.979 --> 00:08:14.889
Loki's possession of the shiny, superpowered
gem brings the catastrophic attention of Thanos

00:08:14.889 --> 00:08:20.330
down upon them, causing destruction and misery
for the Asgardians and the death of Loki himself.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.610
While a decent case could be made for the
desire to nab an Infinity Stone when it's

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:28.159
just lying there, Loki's actions once again
came back to bite him — and his people,

00:08:28.159 --> 00:08:31.129
and ultimately the universe itself, in a supremely
painful manner.

00:08:32.180 --> 00:08:33.990
You'd think Loki would have learned the first
time.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:35.180
Or the second.

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.250
You'd think that, but you'd be wrong.

00:08:37.250 --> 00:08:40.800
Loki's first Tesseract burglary happens in
"Avengers," then he gets his hands on the

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:44.620
Infinity Stone a second time in "Ragnarok,"
which leads to his untimely death.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:48.930
Fortunately, "untimely" is exactly how he
pops up with the Tesseract a third time, not

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.820
in the future, but in the past.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.380
When the Avengers go back in time to gather
up Infinity Stones in "Endgame," they initially

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:58.470
botch the job when the Tesseract is dropped
right in front of the captured 2012 version

00:08:58.470 --> 00:08:59.470
of Loki.

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:03.750
Seeing an opportunity for mischief, Loki snatchs
the Infinity Stone a third time, disappearing

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:06.699
into his own spin-off Disney+ show.

00:09:06.699 --> 00:09:10.350
While 2012 Loki's story is a fun side effect
of the "Endgame" storyline, it doesn't change

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.850
the fact that it was a nasty twist for Earth's
Mightiest Heroes.

00:09:13.850 --> 00:09:17.940
The sudden absence of the Tesseract leaves
the team scrambling, as Loki's initial getaway

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:20.240
nearly upsets their entire time heist.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.899
If they hadn't found an alternative, Loki's
actions could have prevented the Blip return

00:09:23.899 --> 00:09:26.889
and left half of the universe permanently
snapped away.

00:09:26.889 --> 00:09:28.660
Where does Loki's story go from there?

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:32.350
Fans are eager to find out — and discover
how many more times this Marvel favorite is

00:09:32.350 --> 00:09:34.670
going to do some very bad things.

